  "fallback": {
    "text": "Both traversals start at A and mark each visited node so it is never processed again. BFS keeps discovered nodes in a FIFO queue. It visits A, then B and C, then D, then E. Because nodes are visited in queue order, BFS gives shortest unweighted distances: A=0, B=1, C=1, D=2, E=3. DFS keeps its frontier in a LIFO stack. From A it goes to B, then D, then E, and only afterward returns to C. Its order is A, B, D, E, C. When C considers its edge to D, D is already visited and is skipped. DFS is not guaranteed to find a shortest path in an unweighted graph; BFS is."
  },
